    "narrative": "For the Zurich Zebras on May 17, our efficiency model highlights **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (76.11) and **Christian Yelich** (73.09) as the clear engines of the offense. **Pete Crow-Armstrong** benefits from a massive 19% bump due to the favorable matchup against Erick Fedde, and while his #8 spot in the order is a drag, his potential for stolen bases\u2014our scarcest category\u2014coupled with the opposing pitcher's slow delivery makes him our highest-upside play. Similarly, **Christian Yelich** leverages a 13% platoon advantage against Bailey Ober to maintain elite status. While **Carson Benge** (67.24) and **Junior Caminero** (61.60) both grade out strongly, the overall lineup consistency is threatened by facing tier-one arms like Paul Skenes, which forces us to temper expectations for **Trea Turner** (50.80) and **Kyle Schwarber** (48.24).\n\nThe most difficult roster decision today involved **Caleb Durbin**, who nearly cracked the lineup with a 52.59 score. Ultimately, his projected #9 spot in the batting order created a significant drag on his efficiency compared to the middle-of-the-pack starters. We are prioritizing the higher lineup placement of **Casey Schmitt** (54.26) and **Kazuma Okamoto** (55.40), as the run-scoring environment for those spots provides a higher floor in a 5x5 Roto context. Managers should monitor weather and final lineups closely, as the \"Lineup Pending\" status across the board\u2014particularly for **Gary Sanchez** and **Dillon Dingler**\u2014introduces volatility. We are currently avoiding **Zac Veen**, who remains in the minors, and we will continue to monitor the performance of **Hyeseong Kim**, **Chase Meidroth**, **Lars Nootbaar**, **Josh Smith**, **Luis Pena**, and **Pedro Ramirez** to ensure our bench depth remains prepared for spot-start opportunities.",
